  - The form ([38;5;153mindex.html[39m) collects only username and password. Nothing in the app produces or holds a userId.

  [1mClassification: bounded[0m — the login flow already exists here, so I'll present a short design in chat rather than write
  a spec. One thing has to be settled first, because it changes what the code looks like.

  [1mThe fork:[0m [38;5;153mlogin[39m is the function that [3mestablishes[0m who the user is, and nothing upstream of it knows a userId. So "pass
  a userId in" needs a source, and the source decides whether a parameter is even the right shape.

  - [1mServer/stub returns the ID[0m — no signature change; [38;5;153mlogin[39m returns [38;5;153m{ success, user, userId }[39m and the caller logs it.
    Matches the real data flow (the auth API is what knows the account ID) and touches no callers. Doesn't match your
    request literally.
  - [1mCaller generates a tracking ID[0m — [38;5;153mlogin(username, password, userId)[39m where the handler makes a [38;5;153mcrypto.randomUUID()[39m per
    attempt. Gives you a correlation ID that exists before the response, useful for tying a failed attempt to a log
    line. But it identifies the [3mattempt[0m, not the person.
  - [1mCaller supplies a real account ID[0m — same signature, but the value has to come from somewhere new: a third form field
    or a lookup. Most work, and it asks the user to type something they usually don't know.

  Cheap to change later in all three; the signature change itself is low-risk since there's exactly one in-repo caller.

  My recommendation is the first: if the goal is knowing [3mwho[0m logged in, the userId belongs in the response, not the
  arguments. Pick the second if what you actually want is correlating one login attempt across log lines.
